# Passwordless sudo for the Clawcius host agent's own service account.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-11, AND WHY THIS FILE IS NOW WORTH READING
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Until 2026-08-11 this file granted its rules to `npurcell` — the operator —
# and the version of it written on 2026-08-10 said so plainly in a paragraph
# headed "be clear about what this does and does not buy":
#
#     It does NOT contain the host agent. It cannot. npurcell is in the
#     `docker` group on this host — SETUP.md says so in the line that adds it,
#     "docker group is effectively root on the host" — and `docker run -v
#     /:/host` is root with no sudo involved at all.
#
# That was honest and it was also fatal to the whole exercise. A carefully
# scoped command alias granted to a member of the docker group is not a rule;
# it is a description of the polite route. Every wildcard below was therefore
# free — `systemctl restart *` could take out sshd, but so could
# `docker run -v /:/host` — and nobody had to think about it.
#
# The host agent now runs as its own unprivileged system account, by default
# `clawcius-ops`, which is deliberately NOT in `docker`, `sudo`, `wheel`,
# `adm`, `disk` or any other root-equivalent group. `ops/src/agent-user.ts`
# REFUSES TO START A SESSION as an account that is, checked against the live
# /etc/group before every single task rather than once at boot — because the
# `usermod -aG docker clawcius-ops` this is guarding against is something
# somebody types on a running host to make a task work.
#
# So for the first time the contents of this file are the actual bound on what
# the session can do, and every wildcard in it had to go.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT THIS FILE IS, AND — STILL — WHAT IT IS NOT
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# It IS:
#
#   1. **The bound on the easy path, and the easy path is the audited one.**
#      Every command the session runs is written into
#      /var/lib/clawcius-ops/journal.jsonl, in full, before its result is
#      known. Granting `ALL` would make "get a root shell" one command;
#      granting these makes the obvious way to do each job the way that reads
#      clearly in the log.
#   2. **A blast-radius limit inside this project's own namespace.** No sshd,
#      no systemd-journald, no nftables/ufw, no docker.service, no `docker
#      run`, no apt. A task that goes wrong in the way tasks go wrong — a typo,
#      a confused model, a badly worded request — cannot take the host's
#      network or its login off the air.
#   3. **A diff.** Widening it is a change somebody reviews, which is the whole
#      point of it living in the repository rather than being typed into visudo
#      at 3am.
#
# It is NOT a boundary against a determined adversary, and here is the precise
# reason, stated rather than left to be discovered:
#
#   **Installing a unit file plus restarting a named unit is root, in two
#   audited steps.** CLAWCIUS_UNITS below lets the agent `install` a file into
#   /etc/systemd/system, and CLAWCIUS_UNITS_RW lets it restart
#   clawcius.service. Overwrite clawcius.service with one whose ExecStart is
#   anything, reload, restart: that is root. There is no way to remove that
#   without also removing the ability to deploy this project's own units, which
#   is the capability the whole ops mechanism exists to provide.
#
#   What it costs the adversary is that both steps are in the journal, in full,
#   fsynced before they ran, and they look like exactly what they are. That is
#   the trade, and it is the same trade the audit log makes everywhere else in
#   this design.
#
# One more, and it is new since the account changed: **any sudo rule naming a
# path inside the checkout would be equivalent to `sudo ALL`.** The agent can
# write /home/npurcell/clawcius (that is the point of the shared group in
# MIGRATION.md § 2), so `sudo /home/npurcell/clawcius/docker/run-container.sh`
# would be "run this file I can edit, as root". That is why there is no rule
# for run-container.sh or snapshot.sh here even though the agent obviously
# needs containers recreated: **the executor runs those two scripts itself, as
# root, with an argv it builds** — see ops/src/executor.ts. Never add a sudo
# rule pointing into a tree this account can write.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# INSTALLING IT
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root \
#     /home/npurcell/clawcius/ops/clawcius-sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/clawcius
#
# CHECK IT PARSES BEFORE YOU RELY ON IT. A syntax error in one file under
# /etc/sudoers.d breaks sudo for everybody, including the shell you would use
# to fix it:
#
#   sudo visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/clawcius
#
# Do that from a SECOND shell that already holds root. See MIGRATION.md § 3.
#
# NOT YET VERIFIED WITH visudo -c. Unchanged from the previous version and
# still true: this was written on a machine with no sudo installed and has
# never been parsed by the real binary.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TWO THINGS ABOUT SUDO'S MATCHING THAT DECIDE THE SHAPE OF THIS FILE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# 1. **Unit names must be written in full, with the suffix.** `systemctl
#    restart clawcius` and `systemctl restart clawcius.service` do the same
#    thing to systemd and are different strings to sudo, and only the second
#    one is listed below. If a command here is refused with "a password is
#    required", the shorthand is the first thing to check. Both spellings are
#    NOT listed, deliberately: the value of this file is that it can be read in
#    one sitting, and doubling it to absorb an abbreviation would cost more
#    than the refusal does.
#
# 2. **`*` matches everything, including `/` and spaces.** sudo joins the
#    arguments into one string and fnmatch()es it without FNM_PATHNAME. So the
#    old `chown … /var/lib/clawcius*` rule matched
#    `/var/lib/clawcius/../../etc`, and the old `systemctl restart *` matched
#    literally any unit. That was documented and shrugged off in the previous
#    version, on the grounds that the docker group made it moot. It is not moot
#    any more, so the paths below are ENUMERATED rather than globbed. Where a
#    `*` survives it is either in a read-only command or in a position where
#    what it matches cannot escape a fixed prefix AND a fixed suffix.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ADDING SOMETHING
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A new unit, a new container or a new state directory means one more line
# here, a `visudo -c`, and a re-install. That cost is deliberate. The 2026-08-10
# version removed the unit list precisely because "the evening was spent
# standing up NEW units, which by definition are not in any list" — and that
# argument is still right about *installing* a unit (which is granted) and
# wrong about *starting* one (which is running new code as root, forever, and
# is the single step most worth a human's attention).

# ── Who ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match `hostAgent.user` in ops-config.yaml. If the two ever disagree the
# symptom is every privileged command in a task failing with a password prompt
# that nothing can answer, which reads like a broken sudoers file rather than
# like a mismatched name.
User_Alias CLAWCIUS_AGENT = clawcius-ops

# ── Environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `env_reset` is sudo's default and is restated because the next line depends
# on it. The `env_delete` additions are not decoration:
#
# `sudo journalctl` will page its output, and a pager is a shell. `less`
# reaches a root shell with `!sh` in one keystroke, and `journalctl` honours
# $SYSTEMD_PAGER and $PAGER to decide which pager to run. In practice the
# session is not a tty and journalctl does not page — but "in practice" and "is
# not a tty" are exactly the phrases that precede an incident in this
# repository, and deleting the variables costs nothing.
Defaults:CLAWCIUS_AGENT env_reset
Defaults:CLAWCIUS_AGENT env_delete += "PAGER SYSTEMD_PAGER SYSTEMD_LESS LESS LESSOPEN LESSCLOSE LESSSECURE EDITOR VISUAL SUDO_EDITOR"
# There is no terminal. Without this, a rule that ever needed a password would
# hang until the task timeout instead of failing in a second with a readable
# message. Every rule here is NOPASSWD, so this is belt and braces.
Defaults:CLAWCIUS_AGENT !requiretty
# CONSIDERED AND REJECTED: `log_input, log_output, iolog_dir=…`. sudo's I/O
# logging would give a second, independent record of the privileged subset that
# this account cannot rewrite — genuinely attractive, since the executor's own
# journal is the only record today. It is not enabled because `sudo journalctl
# -u clawcius -n 5000` would then be written to disk twice, in full, on a host
# whose known gaps already include ~2 GB snapshot images, and a full disk turns
# every one of this daemon's recovery paths into a failure at once. sudo's
# ordinary syslog line (who ran what, as whom) is on by default and is the
# cheap 90% of the value. Revisit if the journal is ever suspected of being
# tampered with.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# systemd — units this project owns, by name
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The mutating verbs. Every unit in `systemd/` is here, plus the two OJ units
# that do not exist yet (naming a unit that is not installed grants nothing and
# saves an edit on the day it is).
#
# ── clawcius-ops.service is DELIBERATELY ABSENT ───────────────────────────
#
# It is the process that starts the host agent. Restarting it sends SIGTERM to
# the session's parent mid-task: the task dies, systemd brings the daemon back
# with an empty queue, the operation is simply gone — no failure, no journal
# entry saying it did not finish — and the audit ends mid-sentence with nothing
# saying why. Worse, the armed rollback deadlines are re-read from disk on
# boot, so a restart during a task's deadline window is a recovery window
# silently restarted.
#
# Until 2026-08-11 this was prevented by a sentence in the standing prompt
# ("You cannot restart clawcius-ops.service. Ask the operator."). An
# instruction can be talked out of; a missing sudoers line cannot. It is now
# both: the prompt still explains WHY, so the refusal the session hits is
# understood rather than routed around.
#
# Reading its status and its journal IS granted, below, because a session
# debugging its own supervisor is useful and cannot break it.
#
# ── docker.service is DELIBERATELY ABSENT ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The 2026-08-10 version noted that removing the unit list had made
# docker.service reachable, and called that "part of the trade". Restarting
# docker takes every container on this host down at once — both agents, mid
# conversation — and there is no task for which that is the smallest change
# that fixes the thing. It is back out of reach.
#
# sshd/ssh, systemd-journald, nftables, ufw, systemd-resolved and the rest of
# the base system are absent for the obvious reason: a confused task that stops
# one of them ends the operator's ability to reach the box, and no ops task has
# ever needed to.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_RESTART = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart oj-container.service

Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_START = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start oj-container.service

Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_STOP = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop oj-container.service

# enable/disable change what happens at the NEXT boot, which is a different and
# slower-acting kind of damage than a restart — a task that disables
# clawcius.service leaves everything working until a reboot nobody connects to
# it. Same list, deliberately, so there is one set of units to reason about.
# reset-failed is here rather than with the read-only verbs because it clears
# state systemd is using to hold a unit down.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_ENABLE = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable oj-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-snapshot.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable clawcius-snapshot-verify.timer, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable oj-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed hamachi.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-status.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed hamachi-container.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-netguard.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-snapshot.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed clawcius-snapshot-verify.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed oj.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed oj-container.service

# daemon-reload takes no unit and changes nothing by itself; it is what makes
# an installed unit file visible. Bare, with no arguments, so it cannot absorb
# one.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_DAEMON_RELOAD = /usr/bin/systemctl daemon-reload

# ── Read-only systemctl, any unit, on purpose ─────────────────────────────
#
# Answering "what is the state of this box" cannot break it, and the whole
# reason this service exists is that the operator was reading unit states out
# loud to an agent that could not see them. Restricting these to the project's
# own units would mean an agent debugging "clawcius.service will not start"
# could not look at the unit it depends on.
#
# The one honest caveat: `systemctl show` prints a unit's inline `Environment=`
# values. None of this project's units carry a secret inline — they use
# EnvironmentFile, and `show` prints only the PATH of those — but a third-party
# unit on this host that inlined a credential would be readable here. Noted
# rather than papered over; unit files under /etc/systemd/system are
# world-readable anyway, so this grants nothing `cat` does not.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNIT_READ = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl status *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-active *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-enabled *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-failed *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl show *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl cat *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl list-units *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl list-timers *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl list-unit-files *, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl --failed, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl ""

# The `""` on the bare entry above is not decoration. A command listed in
# sudoers with NO argument spec permits that command with ANY arguments — so
# a bare `/usr/bin/systemctl` line grants `systemctl restart sshd.service`,
# `systemctl stop` anything, and every other invocation the careful list above
# it spells out one unit at a time. `""` means "no arguments", which is what
# was intended: bare `systemctl` lists units.
#
# Found on 2026-08-12 by the operator running the step-3 refusal check:
# `sudo -u clawcius-ops sudo -n /usr/bin/systemctl restart sshd.service`
# returned "Unit sshd.service not found" rather than a refusal — sudo had
# permitted it and systemctl merely had nothing to restart. The enumeration
# had been decorative from the moment it was written.

# `systemctl edit` and `set-property` are NOT here and never should be: `edit`
# opens $EDITOR as root, which is a root shell with extra steps, and the
# env_delete line above exists partly so that a future mistake in this file
# does not immediately become one.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# journalctl — the thing that started all of this
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The specific pain point: the operator was reading `journalctl -u …` output
# out loud to an agent that could not see it. Read-only, any unit, for the same
# reason as the read-only systemctl verbs.
#
# `--file`/`--directory` are not excluded and cannot usefully be: they only
# read journal files, which is what this grant is for. The pager escape is
# handled by the env_delete line above.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_LOGS = /usr/bin/journalctl "", /usr/bin/journalctl *

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# docker — named containers, read-mostly, and NOT `docker run`
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# This section did not exist before 2026-08-11. It could not: the previous
# grantee reached docker through group membership, so a sudoers rule "would add
# nothing except the false impression that docker access is being controlled
# here". The agent account is not in the docker group, so these rules are now
# the entire docker capability the session has, and every line is a decision.
#
# NOT GRANTED, and each for its own reason:
#
#   * `docker run` / `docker create` — this is THE hole. `docker run -v /:/host
#     alpine chroot /host sh` is root, immediately, with no audit inside it.
#     Nothing the host agent legitimately does needs it: the two containers on
#     this host are created by docker/run-container.sh, which the EXECUTOR runs
#     as root with an argv it builds itself.
#   * `docker exec` — `docker exec -u root clawcius-agent …` is root inside a
#     container that bind-mounts /var/lib/clawcius/run. That directory is the
#     ops spool, and writing a request into another instance's spool is forging
#     provenance, which is the one property per-instance spools exist to make
#     unforgeable.
#   * `docker cp` — copies as root, to and from arbitrary host paths. It is
#     `sudo cp` wearing a container.
#   * `docker build` / `load` / `import` / `commit` / `save` — a build runs a
#     Dockerfile as root, and the rest move image layers around. The snapshot
#     path (`docker commit`) belongs to the executor, which takes one before
#     every task without being asked.
#   * `docker system prune`, `volume rm`, `network rm` — destructive, and the
#     rollback is container-scoped, so a pruned image is a rollback target that
#     no longer exists.
#
# The read-only verbs take a `*` because their arguments are flags and format
# strings, and because there is nothing on the far side of them to protect: the
# information is the point of the grant.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_READ = \
    /usr/bin/docker ps, \
    /usr/bin/docker ps *, \
    /usr/bin/docker images, \
    /usr/bin/docker images *, \
    /usr/bin/docker version, \
    /usr/bin/docker info, \
    /usr/bin/docker stats --no-stream, \
    /usr/bin/docker stats --no-stream *, \
    /usr/bin/docker inspect *, \
    /usr/bin/docker container inspect *, \
    /usr/bin/docker image inspect *, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs * clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs * hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker logs * oj-agent

# Restarting a container kills whatever turn it was in the middle of, which is
# why the executor waits for an idle turn before anything destructive. The
# session gets these because "the agent container is wedged" is a real thing a
# task is asked to fix, and because the deadline machinery notices a container
# name in the audit and arms a check-in for it.
#
# There is no `docker rm`. Removing an agent container discards its writable
# layer, and the way to get one back is the executor's rollback path, not a
# task's.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_LIFECYCLE = \
    /usr/bin/docker restart clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker restart hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker restart oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker stop clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker stop hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker stop oj-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker start clawcius-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker start hamachi-agent, \
    /usr/bin/docker start oj-agent

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# State directories — enumerated, because `*` matches `..`
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The 2026-08-10 version scoped these by path glob (`/var/lib/clawcius*`) and
# documented, honestly, that `chown … /var/lib/clawcius/../../etc` matches —
# concluding that "on a host where npurcell is already in the docker group that
# changes nothing about what is reachable". That conclusion was correct and its
# premise is gone, so the glob had to go with it. These are exact paths.
#
# Most of this is repair work rather than routine: the executor creates and
# chowns every spool and the host agent's working directory at boot
# (`ensureSpoolDir` / `ensureDirOwnedBy`), precisely so that an agent whose
# requests silently vanish is not a state anybody has to discover. These rules
# are for the case where that went wrong, and for standing up a new instance.
#
# Note the absence of /home/npurcell: the agent shares that tree through a
# group, not through sudo, and a `sudo chown` inside it is how the 2026-08-09
# root-owned-node_modules incident got "fixed" twice. Note also the absence of
# /var/lib/clawcius-ops: it holds journal.jsonl, the circuit breaker and the
# armed deadlines. No rule here reaches it, and for the first time that is a
# statement about what the session can do rather than a statement about what it
# would have to bother doing.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_STATE_MKDIR = \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/clawcius, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/clawcius/run, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/clawcius/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/clawcius/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/hamachi, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/hamachi/run, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/hamachi/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/hamachi/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/oj, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/oj/run, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/oj/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/oj/run/wake

# The uid:gid pairs are spelled out rather than matched with a character class,
# because `[a-z]*\:[a-z]*` accepts `root:root` and the whole question this rule
# answers is "who ends up owning the spool". npurcell is the answer for the
# instance state directories, because that is the uid the containers run as
# (the Dockerfile builds the agent user with AGENT_UID=1000 to match).
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_STATE_CHOWN = \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/clawcius/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/clawcius/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/hamachi/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/hamachi/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/oj/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/chown npurcell\:npurcell /var/lib/oj/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/clawcius/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/clawcius/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/hamachi/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/hamachi/run/wake, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/oj/run/ops, \
    /usr/bin/chmod 0770 /var/lib/oj/run/wake

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Unit files
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# `install` rather than `cp` because it takes the mode and ownership as
# arguments, so the rule can pin them: a unit file arrives 0644 root:root or it
# does not arrive.
#
# The destination is namespaced. The previous version's `/etc/systemd/system/*`
# would have accepted `sshd.service.d/override.conf` — `*` matches `/` — and
# the whole base system with it. These accept only names beginning with this
# project's prefixes, and the trailing `*` cannot climb out of the directory it
# is anchored in because there is no `..` that produces a path still ending in
# a project-prefixed unit name... which is a sentence worth distrusting, so:
# the honest version is that this is a much narrower `*` than the old one and
# it is still a `*`. Combined with `systemctl daemon-reload` and a restart of a
# named unit, it is root — see the note at the top. It is granted anyway
# because installing this project's units is the capability the ops mechanism
# was built to provide, and it is in the audit.
Cmnd_Alias CLAWCIUS_UNITS = \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 -o root -g root * /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 -o root -g root * /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.timer, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 -o root -g root * /etc/systemd/system/hamachi*.service, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 -o root -g root * /etc/systemd/system/oj*.service, \
    /usr/bin/rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.service, \
    /usr/bin/rm -f /etc/systemd/system/clawcius*.timer, \
    /usr/bin/rm -f /etc/systemd/system/hamachi*.service, \
    /usr/bin/rm -f /etc/systemd/system/oj*.service

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# What is deliberately NOT here
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# * Any blanket interpreter: no `sudo sh`, `bash`, `python3`, `perl`, `env`,
#   `tee`, `cp`, `dd`, `mv`, `ln`, `vi`, `find`, `awk`, `xargs`. Those are the
#   classic one-line escalations, and each would also be a one-line way to
#   rewrite the audit log that stands where the verb allowlist used to.
# * apt / npm / pip as root. Installing packages system-wide has a blast radius
#   beyond this host's lifetime and is one of the very few things worth waiting
#   for a human on. A task that needs a system package should say so in its
#   report and stop.
# * visudo, usermod, useradd, gpasswd, groupadd, passwd, chsh, su. Granting the
#   ability to grant is the end of the exercise — and `usermod -aG docker
#   clawcius-ops` is now, specifically and by name, the thing this account must
#   not be able to do to itself.
# * setfacl / chattr. Both are ways to reach the operator's files past the mode
#   bits that ops/src/agent-user.ts checks.
# * mount / umount. A bind mount of /home somewhere the mode-bit check does not
#   look would defeat the secret-readability assertion silently.
# * /var/lib/clawcius-ops, in any rule, by any path.
# * docker.service, sshd, systemd-journald, the firewall, and everything else
#   in the base system. See the systemd section.

CLAWCIUS_AGENT ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: CLAWCIUS_UNIT_RESTART, CLAWCIUS_UNIT_START, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_UNIT_STOP, CLAWCIUS_UNIT_ENABLE, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_DAEMON_RELOAD, CLAWCIUS_UNIT_READ, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_LOGS, CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_READ, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_DOCKER_LIFECYCLE, CLAWCIUS_STATE_MKDIR, \
                                    CLAWCIUS_STATE_CHOWN, CLAWCIUS_UNITS
